But there is something odd about asking someone to express on a scale. =20
Presumably, he has already expressed his degree of happiness in some way, s=
ince=20
that is what we are asking about -- refine your expression.  But under thes=
e=20
conditions what we are asking for in not actually an expression but informa=
tion,=20

we want to know a fact.  And that just isn't what UI (and UICAI) are about.=
  If=20
I stop and consider whether to say 'uicai' or 'uicu'i' or 'uinai' or decide=
 to=20
stick with just 'ui' I am no longer expressing my happiness in any natural =
sense=20

of the word, but rather describing it.  If I burst in the room and say "uic=
ai, I=20

passed", then I am probably expressing my extreme happiness.  If, on the ot=
her=20
hand, I stop to analyze my feelings and then say 'uicai' I am more likely=
=20
seeking to give information -- especially if I do it in answer to a questio=
n.=20
 And UICAI is not about giving information.




